**Proof.** The statement is **true**: $A^2 \le A$ implies $0 \le A \le I$.

Since $A$ is a bounded self-adjoint operator on a Hilbert space $\mathcal H$, by the **spectral theorem** there exists a measure space $(X,\mu)$, a unitary operator $U:\mathcal H \to L^2(X,\mu)$, and a real-valued function $f\in L^\infty(X,\mu)$ such that
$$
A = U^{-1} M_f U,
$$
where $M_f$ is the multiplication operator $(M_f g)(x)=f(x)g(x)$ on $L^2(X,\mu)$.

Because $A$ is self-adjoint, $f$ is real-valued (a.e.). Because $A$ is bounded, $f\in L^\infty$.

Now $A^2 = U^{-1} M_{f^2} U$, so the hypothesis $A^2\le A$ — equivalently $A - A^2\ge 0$, i.e. $\langle (A-A^2)v,v\rangle \ge 0$ for all $v$ — translates to
$$
M_{f - f^2} \ge 0.
$$
A multiplication operator $M_g$ on $L^2(X,\mu)$ is positive if and only if $g(x)\ge 0$ for $\mu$-a.e. $x$. (Indeed, testing against $g = \mathbf 1_E$ for measurable sets $E$ of finite measure, or more directly: if $g<0$ on a set of positive measure, one constructs an $L^2$ function supported there giving a negative quadratic form; the converse is immediate.)

Therefore
$$
f(x)-f(x)^2 = f(x)\bigl(1-f(x)\bigr) \ge 0 \quad\text{for }\mu\text{-a.e. }x.
$$
Since $f$ is real-valued, $f(x)(1-f(x))\ge 0$ holds exactly when
$$
0 \le f(x) \le 1 \quad\text{for }\mu\text{-a.e. }x.
$$

Consequently:
- $f\ge 0$ a.e. $\Rightarrow$ $M_f\ge 0$ $\Rightarrow$ $A\ge 0$.
- $1-f\ge 0$ a.e. $\Rightarrow$ $M_{1-f}\ge 0$ $\Rightarrow$ $I-A = U^{-1}M_{1-f}U \ge 0$ $\Rightarrow$ $A\le I$.

Combining, $0\le A\le I$, i.e. $0\le \langle Av,v\rangle\le \langle v,v\rangle$ for all $v\in\mathcal H$. $\blacksquare$

